\description{
This function combines the utilisation distributions (UDs) of individual
tracks estimated with \code{\link[=hr_tt_akde]{hr_tt_akde()}} into population-level UDs, using the
methods of the package \code{ctmm}. If \code{x} is grouped (e.g. by a variable of the
metadata, such as sex or colony, joined to the table of UDs), a population
UD is estimated for each group; otherwise, all the UDs are combined into a
single population UD.
}
\details{
Two methods are available:
\itemize{
\item \code{"pkde"} (the default): population kernel density estimation
(\code{\link[ctmm:pkde]{ctmm::pkde()}}), which estimates the population UD from the locations
of all the tracks of a group, using kernels based on the movement models
of the individuals. This requires the tracks (\code{tracks}) and the models
(\code{models}) used to estimate the individual UDs.
\item \code{"mean"}: the (weighted) average of the individual UDs
(\code{ctmm::mean.UD()}). The UD is the same as the one obtained with
\code{\link[=hr_tt_ud_sum]{hr_tt_ud_sum()}}, but the \code{ctmm} UD object includes a population model,
which is used to estimate the confidence intervals of the population home
range area. With \code{sample = TRUE} (the default), the tracks are considered
a sample of a larger population, and the uncertainty of the population
distribution is taken into account.
}

In both cases, the uncertainty of the population home range areas
reflects the variation among individuals, and it is large for groups
with few individuals (as shown by small values of \code{dof_area}).

\code{ctmm} estimates some of the population-level parameters (e.g. the
bandwidth of the population kernels with \code{method = "pkde"}) with a
stochastic optimiser, so the results can differ between runs. With
\code{method = "pkde"}, both the population UD and the confidence intervals of
its area can differ; with \code{method = "mean"}, the population UD is always
the same, but the confidence intervals of its area (and \code{dof_area}) can
differ. Set the seed of the random number generator (with \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}})
before calling \code{hr_tt_akde_pop()} to get reproducible results.

Use groups of at least three individuals: with both methods, \code{ctmm} fits
a population model to the movement models of the individuals, whose
parameters are poorly determined with only two individuals, so that its
optimiser can fail to converge and run for a very long time (depending on
the seed). If a call runs for much longer than expected, add individuals
to the group if possible, or try another seed.

Population kernel density estimation assumes that the individuals of a
group are a sample from a single population, whose home ranges are
distributed around a common centre (following a Gaussian distribution).
Groups of individuals in distinct areas (e.g. different colonies) should
be estimated separately (e.g. by grouping \code{x} by colony): pooling them
leads to a very smooth population UD, which can miss the areas used by
a minority of individuals.

All the UDs of a group must be estimated on the same grid (as is the case
for the UDs returned by a single call of \code{\link[=hr_tt_akde]{hr_tt_akde()}} without a \code{bbox}
for each group), and the population UD is estimated on that grid. With
\code{method = "pkde"}, the kernels of the population are wider than those of
the individuals, so the population UD can extend beyond the grid of the
individual UDs; in this case, the grid is extended (by whole cells, so
that it stays aligned with the original grid, and in the same way for all
groups that shared it) to contain the population UD.
}
